nts don't read anymore  Martin Luther King assumed an audience  that actually was educated well enough  to follow arguments and if you don't  have that it's just a lot of gobbly [ __ ]  to you with a few you know Pius  sentiments thrown in if you actually can  look at arguments and it takes a lot of  practice to be able to do that to be  able to understand what an author is  doing if you can do that then are done  it opens up an entire world for you the  whole world of the humanities really  depends on people being able to think  not just appreciate but but think and so  critical thinking yeah we ought to be  teaching our kids that it ought to be a  deliberately infused part of the  curriculum right now it's not I don't  know if that's going to change in the  future  yes I think critical thinking should be  taught in high school and I think it  should be taught in two different ways I  think that it should actually be taught  as portions of a course modules you  might say and I think that it should be  infused into the curriculum in high  school as well one thing I find is that  education people that I deal with they  have an understanding of critical  thinking that does not match up to or  measure up to what we philosophers  actually mean by critical thinking and  if you want to know what critical  thinking means the best place to go is  the american philosophical association  delphi report and in other countries  actually they do teach a lot of what we  call critical thinking in their their  equivalents of high school so i think  that we ought to be doing that i'm not  sure how you would actually get it in  there they would take quite a bit of  work but i think that that's something  that we really need because one of the  things I'm finding is students are  coming students are coming into my  critical thinking classes and they are  just not prepared at all so we have to  do a lot of remedial work  so here's a question from one of my  students do you think that critical  thinking is mainly common sense and the  answer's no I think that critical  thinking and common sense are connected  in very interesting ways I'm going to  try to be really short here so here's  what I have to say critical thinking  goes beyond common sense it builds on it  and I think it presupposes it but  critical thinking as a discipline means  the study of argumentation learning how  to analyze arguments that means break  them into their parts how to evaluate  them tell whether they're good arguments  or bad arguments how to look to see  whether premises or starting points of  arguments are actually true or not how  to evaluate information sources there's  more to critical thinking than that but  you notice that goes beyond just common  sense and it goes beyond common sense  because it presupposes it but it also  goes beyond it because there's certain  methods and common sense doesn't really  have methods I think that common sense  is something that you really have to  have some mo to come up in order to be  able to do any critical thinking  so here's another question good question  about critical thinking and common sense  what is the difference between critical  thinking and common sense so when I  thought about this I came up with three  what is that critical thinking actually  builds on common sense to some degree  and it codifies it and it extends it so  if you don't have some degree of common  sense the critical thinking might not do  you quite that much good the critical  thinking goes beyond common size so  that's one thing second one I notice you  can have a critical thinking textbook do  you have a common sense textbook no  because common sense is sort of a set of  habits that are that are built up  situationally over time critical  thinking can actually be taught because  it consists in some techniques and  concepts procedures and definitions  that's why it's tied in logic logic can  be taught to third thing common sense  can't actually take 