u'll  continue with what you've learned in the  class was of course assuming that you've  actually put in the necessary work to  have gotten much out of the class which  I would say only a third of my students  do in any given semester what are you  supposed to be you're supposed to build  upon the foundation that we worked with  in the class and you're supposed to  extend it and you're supposed to make  this part of your set of tools or you  might think of it as part of your  skeleton of your thought you know  there's a lot of areas in which critical  thinking and the skills and the  dispositions that it comprises like you  know for instance giving reasons that  other people might find convincing for  the claims that you're making or looking  for other people to give reasons  similarly that that's a habit that's not  something that you just automatically  grasp and start doing you've got to  build that up as part of your or your  repertoire your way of communicating  with other people your way of being I  hope that you do build on it because I  think this stuff is really important and  I can pick out four main areas where  it's critically important one is in your  other classes you know you need to be  able to make arguments to look at other  people's arguments to evaluate  information sources to compare positions  against each other to give explanations  for what's going on and also reducing  self scrutiny and so that you can prove  politics is another really important  area we're  where you have to apply critical  thinking and the marketplace you know as  a consumer you need to be able to apply  critical thinking and then your personal  life a lot of arguments that people get  into when you look at them carefully a  lot of what therapists are actually  doing is pointing out errors and  critical thinking that they're your  clients are making so that's why it's  important  do I think that students we use critical  thinking after they've left my class I  know that some of them do because I do  have students who come up to me and  it'll be out of the blue they see me on  campus and they say hey that that thing  that we learned in your class this topic  I ended up using it in another class or  you know I was watching the news and and  I realized that they weren't giving me  the whole story because or I was tempted  to buy this product but then I started  looking closely at the advertisement and  analyzing it so I know that people are  using some of the things they have to do  they leave the class and a lot of the  students have actually said that the  focus on argument and being able to make  arguments to compose them really helped  them a lot in their writing in other  classes my hope of course as an educator  is that all the students continue to  practice what we learn in the critical  thinking classes the reality is I think  most of the students actually just get  through the class and then forget about  it which is terrible because when it  comes to voting consuming being  productive members of society they  they're at a disadvantage and they're  actually going to be a problem for their  fellow citizens family members they're  contributing to a marketplace where  people are able to exploit each other  very very easily critical thinking is  actually you might say kind of a duty  for an educated person so that's why we  teach it that's why it's a required  class of Fayetteville State University  it's not in a lot of places should be  should be required everywhere but  unfortunately it's not  worse if you read your textbook on page  36 that's recovered this right now yeah  this is from one of my critical thinking  students and it's a very legitimate  question how does critical thinking  prepare us for the world outside of  college and you know I spend some of the  class time talking about how what we're  doing in the critical thinking class  which deals with building blocks of  arguments how to analyze arguments how  to evaluate arguments how to look at  larger arguments with information  sources how to evaluate them with issues  of bias the informal fallacies and then  with some of the basics of inductive  techniques of inductive reasoning I  often stress that this is really useful  for your other classes but that's a  legitimate question what good is this  stuff once we actually get out of  college and I think that you know if you  look at the examples in in my course  lectures that I've put online for for  you guys you notice that I give a lot of  useful examples not from other classes  but from real life ranging from politics  to products to things about stereotyping  personal relationships all those sorts  of things usually when we make bad  choices bad inferences when we get into  heated arguments with people it's  because some sort of failure of critical  thinking has taken place and it may not  be right there in that that there we go  right there in the argument that you're  looking at it may be of a larger context  and this actually some type of fusion  few seconds left critical thinking is  not just a set of techniques it's not  just a set of skills it's not just  knowledge it's also dispositions and  that disposition to look at the larger  context and to self-evaluate that's  really core to critical thinking so and  that's important in life  what is a good deductive argument well  this one is actually very  straightforward and simple to answer a  good deductive argument is one which is  both valid and sound so valid means that  it has a good structure the nice thing  about deductive arguments is you can  analyze them solely in terms of their  structure if you want to and that's what  formal logic is is all about you can  symbolize them with with you know  various symbols that we use and with  letters or any other kind of variables  that you like and you can look just at  the form and if the premises being true  would lead to the conclusion being true  then it's valid and there'